---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TRUST_LADDER } from '../lib/trust.js';

const reportError = 'https://github.com/nishachay/closed-form/issues/new';
---
<Base title="About" description="Why Closed Form exists, what the trust labels mean, and how entries are made.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="wrap-article" style="margin-inline: 0">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1 class="page">AI mathematics, explained — honestly.</h1>

      <h2>Why Closed Form exists</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        AI systems now produce mathematics faster than anyone can read it. The papers are written for
        specialists, while the public sees only headlines. Closed Form is a calm public archive that turns
        each result into a page a curious adult can read in three minutes: what it claims, how sure we can
        be, where the question came from, and what changes if it is true.
      </p>

      <h2>The trust labels</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Every entry carries a trust label, computed from the source data — never written by AI, never
        hidden, never softened.
      </p>
      <ul class="rows">
        {TRUST_LADDER.map((t) => (
          <li class="row">
            <div class="row-link" style="cursor: default">
              <span class="row-id" />
              <span>
                <span class="row-title">{t.label}</span>
                <span class="row-summary">
                  {t.key === 'formal' &&
                    'At least one paper in the family is listed in the Lean formalization catalogue.'}
                  {t.key === 'partial' &&
                    'No listed formal proof, but the family has Lean notes.'}
                  {t.key === 'claimed' && 'Neither — the claim has not been machine-checked.'}
                </span>
              </span>
              <span class="row-trust" />
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="measure">
        “Formal proof listed” means the repository lists a formalization — not that it compiles, and not
        that the Lean statement matches the paper. That is why the label says <em>listed</em>, not{' '}
        <em>verified</em>.
      </p>

      <h2>Review status</h2>
      <ul class="measure">
        <li>Sample entry, written by hand</li>
        <li>AI draft — not checked yet (never published)</li>
        <li>Checked by AI against the paper — not yet reviewed by a person</li>
        <li>Reviewed by a person, with a name and date</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="measure">
        Any entry not marked reviewed carries the notice: “This entry has not been reviewed by a person
        yet.” An AI never marks an entry reviewed — only a person does.
      </p>

      <h2>How entries are made</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        A deterministic ingest reads the source manuscripts and builds the catalogue. A reader model drafts
        each explainer from the paper itself; a second model checks every factual claim against the source
        and deletes what it cannot support. Validation enforces the writing rules, and a person reviews
        before any entry counts as reviewed.
      </p>

      <h2>What we don’t do</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        No proof sketches. No hype. No calling a result true because a company published it. No impact the
        paper doesn’t support. The original papers are always one click away — the explainer is a
        translation, not a replacement.
      </p>

      <h2>Report an error</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Found a mistake? <a href={reportError}>Report an error on GitHub</a> — it is the cheapest form of
        human review.
      </p>

      <h2>Credits and license</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Our explanations are published under CC BY 4.0. Source papers come from openai/math (Apache-2.0)
        and remain under their own license. Closed Form is not affiliated with OpenAI.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
